<h3>How the Star Wheel Mechanism Works</h3>
<p>A star wheel is a rotating disc with evenly spaced semicircular pockets around its edge. Each pocket holds one container. The wheel turns in step with the rotary machine it feeds, so one pocket meets one machine station. As the wheel turns, each pocket picks a container off the infeed conveyor, carries it across a stationary dead plate while a curved center guide keeps it seated in the pocket, and sets it into the machine station (a filler valve, a capper chuck, a labeling station). On the outfeed side, a second star wheel takes each container out of the machine and puts it on the discharge conveyor.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/starwheel-septimatech-filler-star.webp" width="1000" height="752" alt="Two red plastic star wheels on a rotary machine, each pocket holding one clear wide-mouth jar, with a curved red guide holding the jars in the pockets." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Septimatech quick change star wheels and guide on a rotary filler and capper, with one jar seated in each pocket. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.septimatech.com/products-and-services/machine-changeovers/change-parts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Star wheels are the bridge between linear conveyor flow and rotary machine motion. Containers arrive in single file on a flat conveyor. The <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed</a> star wheel turns that linear stream into rotary motion, and the outfeed star wheel turns it back into linear flow.</p>
<p>Pocket geometry must match the container. A pocket that is too large lets the container shift and arrive at the station off-center. A pocket that is too small jams the container or damages its wall. That fit is why star wheels are container-specific change parts: a different container diameter or profile usually needs a different star wheel. Ask the supplier what pocket clearance they design for your container material and finish.</p>
<h2 id="screw-heading">Timing Screws Feed the Star Wheel</h2>
<p>A timing screw (also called a feed screw, worm, or scroll) turns beside the infeed conveyor and spaces touching containers apart. Its pitch widens along its length, and at the discharge it equals the star wheel pocket pitch, so each container reaches an open pocket. This is how containers are <a href="/conveyor-functions/metering/">metered</a> into rotary machines. Pitch design, single and dual screws, materials, and troubleshooting are covered in the <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/timing-screw/">timing screw guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="changeover-heading">Change Parts &amp; Changeover</h2>
<p>Star wheels are container-specific change parts. Each container diameter and profile needs its own matched set of star wheels, center guide, timing screw, and guide rails, so a line running five container sizes needs five sets. Mounting design, storage, and changeover method are covered in <a href="/components/change-parts/">conveyor change parts and quick-change systems</a>.</p>
<h2 id="quick-change">Quick-Change Star Wheel Systems</h2>
<p>A quick-change star wheel mounts to a hub that stays on the machine and locks with a hand knob, cam lock, locating pin, or sliding carriage instead of bolts, so it drops into a fixed, repeatable position with no timing adjustment. <a href="https://www.nerconconveyors.com/Nercon-Blog/Star-Wheel-Innovation-Results-in-Faster-Product-Changeover" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nercon</a> describes a bolted fixture swap that "can take a maintenance person 30 minutes to an hour," against star wheels on a pinned sliding carriage that slide into place "within seconds." For mounting methods, published changeover times, and a retrofit checklist for existing equipment, see <a href="/components/change-parts/#retrofit-heading">adding quick-change parts to existing equipment</a>.</p>
<h2 id="sync-heading">Synchronizing the Screw, Star Wheel &amp; Machine</h2>
<p>The timing screw, star wheel, and rotary machine must turn in exact step, linked by gearing or by servos that follow the machine's master encoder. A container that arrives early or late hits the solid section between pockets, or leaves a pocket and machine station empty. Drive options, phase setting, and keyed mounting are covered under <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/timing-screw/#drive-heading">timing screw drive and synchronization</a>.</p>
<h2 id="container-heading">Container Handling</h2>
<p>Star wheels and timing screws are built mainly for round containers. Square, rectangular, or irregular containers need shaped screws and pockets or a different infeed, such as servo-driven lugs, bucket conveyors, or indexing belts. See <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed and outfeed systems</a> for the alternatives.</p>
<p>Container material affects screw design. Glass is heavy and rigid and tolerates contact pressure but can chip from impact. PET bottles are light and flexible and deform under excess screw pressure, which causes uneven spacing. Thin-walled aluminum cans dent easily, so screws and pockets that contact them are usually made from engineering plastics such as UHMW polyethylene or acetal rather than metal.</p>
<p>Container stability is the main limit on line speed. Tall, narrow containers with high centers of gravity tip at lower speeds than short, wide ones. The screw profile, guide rail height, and belt friction must all support the container at the target speed. Empty lightweight PET bottles are the hardest: they blow over from air currents, tip from vibration, and float on wet conveyors.</p>
<h2 id="applications-heading">Industry Applications</h2>
<h3>Beverage Filling Lines</h3>
<p>Beverage lines are the largest application. High-speed rotary fillers set the pace: Krones rates its Modulfill Bloc FS-C at up to 135,000 cans per hour (about 2,250 per minute). At these rates, screw and star wheel precision and fast changeover drive line efficiency. Multi-start timing screws (two or three helical flights on one screw) increase throughput without raising screw speed.</p>
<h3>Pharmaceutical Bottle Lines</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical bottle lines usually run slower than beverage lines but demand cleanability and documented control. Change parts are typically stainless steel or approved plastics with sanitary finishes, and tool-less disassembly supports cleaning validation. Where serialization applies, the transfer must keep containers in sequence so each one stays matched to its record.</p>
<h3>Capping and Labeling Machine Infeed</h3>
<p>Rotary cappers and labelers use star wheels the same way fillers do. Capping star wheels must place the container directly under the cap delivery point. Small misalignment causes cross-threaded or missed caps. Labeling transfers must present the container consistently for label registration, sometimes with an orientation device at the transfer.</p>
<SpecChecklist id="spec-checklist" title="Star wheel and change parts specification checklist" items={[
  ["Containers", "Drawings or samples of every container: diameter, height, shoulder and base profile, material, and empty and filled weight."],
  ["Machine interface", "Machine make and model, pitch circle diameter, pocket or station count, direction of rotation, and handoff point."],
  ["Rates", "Containers per minute at current and planned peak speed, and minimum stable speed for startup."],
  ["Infeed condition", "How containers arrive (touching, back-pressured, or spaced) and the conveyor width, height, and belt type feeding the screw."],
  ["Screw configuration", "Single or dual screw, number of starts, and any turning, grouping, or orienting the screw must do."],
  ["Change parts list", "Which parts are container-specific: screw, infeed and outfeed star wheels, center guide, guide rails, and dead plates."],
  ["Changeover target", "Target changeover time, tool-less or tooled, number of operators, and how parts are located and verified."],
  ["Drive and sync", "Mechanical gearing or servo, master encoder source, and controls platform for cam profiles and recipes."],
  ["Materials and sanitation", "Contact materials, food or drug contact requirements, washdown or dry cleaning, and finish requirements."],
  ["Storage", "Change part carts or racks, labeling, and spare sets."],
  ["Safety", "Guarding of rotating parts and nip points and access for changeover without defeating guards."],
  ["Acceptance test", "Run test with your containers at rated speed, measured changeover time, and allowed jam or tip rate."]
]} />
