<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a star wheel on a packaging line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A star wheel (starwheel or rotary star wheel) is a rotating wheel with evenly spaced pockets that takes round containers one at a time from a conveyor and places them into the stations of a rotary filler, capper, or labeler. A second star wheel takes containers back out onto the outfeed conveyor. The wheel turns in sync with the machine, one pocket per station, and a timing screw upstream spaces containers so each arrives at an open pocket.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a timing screw on a bottle line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A timing screw (worm screw, feed screw, or scroll) is a helical screw that separates touching containers into evenly spaced units. Its pitch widens along its length, and at the discharge it matches the star wheel pocket spacing. See the <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/timing-screw/">timing screw guide</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What are change parts for star wheels and timing screws?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Change parts are container-specific components swapped when switching container sizes: the timing screw, star wheels, center guide, and guide rails. Each size needs its own set. Bolted parts need tools and adjustment, while quick-change parts lock into a fixed position. See <a href="/components/change-parts/">conveyor change parts</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do you synchronize a timing screw with a rotary filler?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Through mechanical gearing or a timing belt from the machine main drive, or a servo that follows the machine master encoder. The discharge pitch must match the star wheel pocket pitch, and the screw must stay in phase. Keyed or pinned mounting keeps the phase correct after changeover.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Can you add quick-change star wheels to existing conveyor equipment?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Often, yes. Retrofits replace bolted star wheel and screw mounts with tool-less hubs, locating pins, or sliding carriages so parts drop into a fixed, repeatable position. Confirm the shaft and hub design, the machine\'s pitch circle and pocket count, guard access, and whether the guides also get quick-change mounts. See the <a href="/components/change-parts/#retrofit-heading">retrofit checklist</a>.</p>

</div>
